    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Mark Twain, as we all know, was a world-famous writer. He was best known for having created the characters, Tom Sawyer and Huckleberry Finn. But in his lifetime, Twain was almost as famous as a public speaker as he was as a writer."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "In 1866, Twain was an unknown reporter. He wrote travel articles for newspapers and had yet to publish any major work. Later that year, Twain travelled to Hawaii. While he was on the island, he wrote about his adventures for newspapers in California. His writings drew public interest. After he got back, Twain was invited to give a speech about his experiences in Hawaii. His sudden popularity shocked him. He wrote about his first speech in his book "
          },
          {
            "t": "em",
            "v": "Roughing It"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ": “I felt so frightened... I pretended to be ill.” However, that first speech was a great success."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Hundreds of public speeches were then arranged across California. They all focused on Twain’s adventures in Hawaii. Twain did not read from his writings when he gave a speech. He always had his audience in mind and carefully prepared for each of his speeches. Twain was not a professional "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "entertainer",
            "zh": "表演者"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", but he tried to entertain his audience. He talked about the customs and habits in Hawaii in an amusing way. As his "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "curtain",
            "zh": "幕布"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " fell, Twain jokingly apologised for having so many people spend time listening to him. “But I need the money!” he would say. The audience would always give Twain a "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "standing ovation",
            "zh": "长时间起立鼓掌"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". People loved his humour so much that no matter where he went, he could fill a lecture hall."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "After his 1866 speech tour, Twain published "
          },
          {
            "t": "em",
            "v": "The Innocents Abroad"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", a book about his travels in Europe. Slowly, his fame grew across the United States."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "By 1872, Twain had grown tired of giving lectures. He hoped he would soon be able to retire from giving these speeches. However, his public appearances greatly helped his book sales, so he returned to the stage again and again. Twain didn’t stop giving speeches until the late 1890s."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
